

The task is to find the node where two linked lists intersect.

The important point is that I need to find the same node, not just two nodes with the same value.

I used two pointers.

The first pointer goes through every node of the first list.

For every node of the first list, the second pointer starts from the beginning of the second list and checks every node.

If:

first == second

then both pointers refer to the same node, so this is the intersection.

If no common node is found, I return null.


Suppose the lists are:

A: 1 → 2 → 8 → 9
B: 4 → 5 → 8 → 9

I first compare node 1 with all nodes of B.

Then I compare node 2 with all nodes of B.

When I reach node 8 in A, I compare it with nodes in B.

The same node 8 is found, so I return it.



Time Complexity: O(n × m)

For every node in the first list, I may check every node in the second list.

Space Complexity: O(1)

I only use two pointer variables.


This solution works, but it is not the most efficient.

A better solution can use two pointers that switch to the other list when they reach the end.

That approach can achieve O(n + m) time and O(1) extra space.

The main thing I would need to change is the way the two pointers move through the lists.
